description: The llvm target's runtime compile cache, part 1: lowered runtime text no longer depends on the program, so it can be cached. Type ids: the static types are the runtime's declarations then FnV and FnX, with the same ids in every compile; program types and closure environments take ids from one counter after them (before, program types sat ahead of FnV and FnX, so a program `deftype` shifted their ids and every environment id). Constant names (`str`, `bytes`, `cstr`) are their kind and the xxh3-64 of their text, not first-use numbers; two texts with one name fail the compile (hex32 had collided). The type table (D218) leaves the runtime's text: `arch/type-*` read `@lg_tinfo_*` by name, with no length or count in the text, and `llvm-module` emits the table and the static Types for each module. `arch/alen` recognises the runtime's array types only, since a program declares none. Why: on 2026-10-09 an llvm compile of `(println 1)` took 20.3 s, about 19.5 s of it analysing and lowering the same runtime for every program (the GC lane caches it, D181; its cold build took 96 s and a warm compile 1.8 s). Behavior is checked, not bytes: the llvm and poly corpora MATCH. Part 2: the llvm lane uses the runtime library cache (D181's `load-rtlib!`, `build-rtlib!`, snapshot and restore; the driver no longer leaves llvm out). Its key adds the four `lower_llvm*.lg` files and the resolved profile to the shared key, which already holds the runtime sources, `lower_wasm.lg` and the lg binary's hash (let-go's IR passes run inside lg); the snapshot adds the llvm session state in `*fn-values*` (environment and program type ids, constant names, the fn-value wrappers and helpers; vars by name), not the derived type lists or the try-region memos. A runtime fn that fails to lower is an llvm stub with its signature that raises its named error (14 in the whole runtime, none of them reached before). With every runtime fn lowered, `llvm-module` shakes: each unit (a lowered fn's text, a region, a global line) carries the symbols it defines and names, found once when it is made and so saved with the cached runtime; liveness grows from `_main`, the program's fns and the prelude's references, a live runtime global bringing its init fn (the hooks global the link step), and the init chain, var globals and collector roots are the live ones. Measured on `(println 1)`, 2026-10-09: an lg compile 20.3 s before, 2.98 s from a warm cache (398 of 3,049 fn units and 202 of 2,716 globals kept; 4.8 MB of IR); clang 1.5 s either way; a cold build 140 s, once per change to the runtime or the compiler. Without the shake the whole runtime reached clang (35.8 MB, 7.4 s). `checks/llvm-rtlib-check.sh` (P12.37) checks behavior and a bound, not bytes: four programs give the same stdout and exit with `--no-rtlib` and from the cache, and a warm compile takes at most 5 s (3.87 s). Part 3 (partial invalidation, a dependency graph with early cutoff) waits; until then any edit to a runtime or llvm compiler file rebuilds the whole library.
